>> title: Brunswick Stew, Slow Cooker
>> category: Stews & Braises
>> tags: pork, chicken, tomato, corn, stew, slow cooker, crock pot
>> counters: The Slow Cooker
>> dish: brunswick-stew
>> kit: Slow Cooker
>> aka: brunswick, bbq stew, barbeque stew, pit stew, camp stew, slow cooker brunswick stew, crockpot brunswick stew
>> pairs-with: skillet-cornbread
>> servings: 8
>> time: 6 hr 40 min
>> slack: forgiving — every piece of meat in this is already cooked, so nothing in the pot is racing anything; the corn is the only thing that goes from sweet to soft, and it is in for the last half hour
>> step.2: sweat 8 min in a skillet
>> step.3: simmer on low, 6 hr
>> step.4: add the corn and limas, 30 min on high
>> step.5: season

Written for low, six hours — shorter than the braises above it, because the pork and the chicken went into this pot already smoked and cooked and the only thing that needs hours is the potato breaking down to thicken it. This is a stew that is better the next day, which is the machine's other quiet argument: it makes the pot you reheat. Stock comes down a quarter only, to three cups, because a Brunswick stew is meant to be loose enough to eat with a spoon.

Sweat @yellow onion{1%large}(diced) in @unsalted butter{2%Tbs}(28 g) in a #skillet{} for ~sweat{8%min}, until it goes soft without colouring.

Slow cook @&(~1)onion{} with @chicken stock{3%cups}(710 mL), @crushed tomatoes{28%oz}(795 g), @cider vinegar{2%Tbs}, @Worcestershire sauce{1%Tbs}, @hot sauce{2%tsp}, @Yukon Gold potatoes{1%lb}(450 g; 1/2-in dice), @chopped smoked pork{12%oz}(340 g), @smoked chicken{12%oz}(340 g; pulled) in a #slow cooker{} on low for ~slow cook{6%hr}, lid on.

Stir @baby lima beans{2%cups}(280 g; frozen), @corn kernels{2%cups}(300 g) into @&(~1)stew{} and hold it on high for ~slow cook{30%min}. Six hours of corn is corn you cannot taste.

Season @&(~1)stew{} with @kosher salt{2%tsp}, @black pepper{1%tsp}, @dark brown sugar{1%Tbs}, tasting for the sweet-sour balance that the vinegar and the sugar are arguing about.
